---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import TemperatureUnits from '../components/TemperatureUnits.astro';
import { climateMonths, weatherSources } from '../data/weatherPlanning';
import '../styles/weatherPlanning.css';
const faqs=[
 {q:'What is the difference between Banff climate and today’s forecast?',a:'Climate normals summarise a historical period. The table here shows ECCC’s 1991–2020 BANFF composite normal daily maximum and minimum temperatures. It is not a forecast, a range of extremes or a promise about a trip date. Use the separate official destination forecast and alerts before an outing.'},
 {q:'Is Lake Louise weather the same as Banff town?',a:'No. Nearby valleys, lakeshores and mountain elevations can have different conditions. Check the forecast for the actual destination and the operator or route report. An observation may come from a nearby station; read the station name as well as the destination label.'},
 {q:'Can I assume summer hiking trails are open from the monthly temperature?',a:'No. Snow, wildlife restrictions, maintenance and weather can affect routes at any time. Read the current Parks Canada trail report and bulletins. A warm day in town does not establish a clear alpine trail.'},
 {q:'Where can I find sunrise and sunset for my travel date?',a:'Use the National Research Council’s calculator for the correct place, date and time zone, including its interpretation notes. Mountain horizons can delay sunlight reaching a viewpoint or hide it before tabulated sunset. A monthly average is not an exact sunrise time.'},
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Banff Weather Guide: Climate, Seasons & How to Plan" description="Understand Banff weather with sourced 1991–2020 temperature normals, seasonal planning advice and the difference between town, Lake Louise and mountain conditions." lastUpdated="2026-10-28">
 <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'FAQPage',mainEntity:faqs.map(f=>({'@type':'Question',name:f.q,acceptedAnswer:{'@type':'Answer',text:f.a}}))})} />
 <div class="container"><Breadcrumbs items={[{label:'Planning Tools',href:'/tools'},{label:'Weather Guide'}]} /></div>
 <section class="weather-hero"><div class="container"><span class="weather-eyebrow">Plan for the place, not just the month</span><h1>Banff weather.<br />A little context helps.</h1><p>Use climate to choose a season, a forecast to prepare for a day, and current route reports to choose an outing. Each answers a different question.</p><nav class="weather-nav" aria-label="Weather planning"><a href="/weather-webcams">Forecasts & webcams →</a><a href="/monthly-weather">Explore your month →</a><a href="/packing-list">Build a packing list →</a></nav></div></section>
 <section class="section"><div class="container"><div class="weather-intro"><span class="weather-eyebrow">Historical context</span><h2>Banff’s normal monthly temperatures</h2><p>These are the normal daily maximum and minimum for ECCC’s <strong>BANFF composite, 1991–2020</strong>. Individual days can be much warmer or colder. These town-area values do not describe a ski summit or every part of the park.</p></div><TemperatureUnits />
 <div class="weather-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Monthly climate table" tabindex="0"><table class="weather-table"><caption>Normal daily maximum and minimum · 1991–2020</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Month</th><th scope="col">Daily maximum</th><th scope="col">Daily minimum</th><th scope="col">Plan the month</th></tr></thead><tbody>{climateMonths.map(m=><tr><th scope="row">{m.name}</th><td data-celsius={m.high}>{m.high.toFixed(1)}°C</td><td data-celsius={m.low}>{m.low.toFixed(1)}°C</td><td><a href={'/monthly-weather#'+m.id}>Explore {m.name} →</a></td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
 <p class="weather-source-note">Source: <a href={weatherSources.normals} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Environment and Climate Change Canada, BANFF 1991–2020 Climate Normals ↗</a>. The composite joins observations from the listed Banff stations. Values reproduced for temperature only; see the complete source for precipitation, snowfall, data coverage and calculation notes. Reviewed 28 October 2026.</p></div></section>
 <section class="section weather-soft"><div class="container"><div class="weather-intro"><h2>Read the season with some flexibility</h2></div><div class="weather-grid"><article class="weather-info-card"><h3>Winter and spring skiing</h3><p>Cold, wind, snow and short daylight shape the day. Resort openings, lift operations, managed rinks and snow tours have their own calendars. A warmer spring town forecast does not remove winter hazards above.</p><a href="/winter">Find a winter day →</a></article><article class="weather-info-card"><h3>Summer outdoors</h3><p>Carry warm layers and weather protection even when town is warm. Check storms, air quality, trail restrictions and the actual transport or attraction opening before committing to a longer outing.</p><a href="/trip-builder?template=first-timer-3">Plan a summer weekend →</a></article><article class="weather-info-card"><h3>Spring and autumn transitions</h3><p>Summer operations may not overlap with winter openings. Choose a confirmed main outing, keep an indoor alternative and avoid treating a season label as proof of road or trail access.</p><a href="/what-to-do-today">Choose a flexible outing →</a></article></div></div></section>
 <section class="section"><div class="container"><div class="weather-copy"><h2>Check three things before leaving</h2><ul><li><strong>The actual destination.</strong> Compare Banff, Lake Louise and the relevant mountain report. Look at the forecast issue time and observation station.</li><li><strong>The complete outing.</strong> Read road, trail, lift or attraction updates and alerts. Do not use a blue-sky camera image as an assessment of a route or natural ice.</li><li><strong>Your group and return.</strong> Match clothing, ability, daylight and the final transport. Leave room to shorten, postpone or change the outing.</li></ul><p><a href={weatherSources.parks} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Parks Canada’s weather guidance</a> explains the variability between nearby locations and through a day. Use the <a href={weatherSources.sun} target="_blank" rel="noopener">NRC sun calculator</a> for your date, with its time-zone and terrain interpretation notes.</p></div><div class="weather-next"><h3>Make the next decision</h3><p>Check a report, pick a realistic day and pack for that outing.</p><nav aria-label="Next weather planning steps"><a href="/weather-webcams">Official forecasts & camera viewers →</a><a href="/ski-day-planner">Plan a ski day →</a><a href="/packing-list">Packing checklist →</a><a href="/conditions">Road and trail reports →</a></nav></div></div></section>
 <section class="section weather-soft"><div class="container"><h2>Weather planning questions</h2><div class="weather-faqs">{faqs.map(f=><details><summary>{f.q}</summary><p>{f.a}</p></details>)}</div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
